<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">First Toungoo Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">1510 CE – 1599 CE · capital Pegu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest empire in the history of Southeast Asia, built by Tabinshwehti and Bayinnaung, which briefly ruled from Manipur to Laos and Ayutthaya.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Toungoo, a small Burmese principality on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sittaung-river">Sittaung</a> river, grew in strength under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingyi-nyo">Mingyi Nyo</a>, who declared independence from Ava in 1510. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a>, from 1530, conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> his capital in 1539 and seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prome-kingdom">Prome</a> and much of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>, king from 1550, took Ava in 1555, subdued the Shan states and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-manipur">Manipur</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a> in 1558 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1564 and 1569, and campaigned in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang</a>. He governed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> rulers bound by oaths and hostages, standardised weights and law, and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> was a wealthy port visited by Portuguese and Venetian traders.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung's</a> death in 1581 the empire fell apart; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naresuan">Naresuan</a> freed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1584, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-bayin">Nanda Bayin's</a> wars exhausted the realm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> was sacked by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mrauk-u-kingdom">Arakan</a> and Toungoo in 1599. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyaungyan-min">Nyaungyan Min</a> rebuilt the state as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/first-toungoo-empire.jpg" alt="First Toungoo Empire" width="320" height="377"><figcaption>Image: Soewinhan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Taungoo_Empire_(1580).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1510 CE – 1599 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pegu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tabinshwehti, Bayinnaung, Nanda Bayin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burmese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>, <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Toungoo dynasty, Second Burmese Empire, Taungoo Empire</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1554&amp;focus=first-toungoo-empire" aria-label="First Toungoo Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7289 1476 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where First Toungoo Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7609 1732L7702 1621M7609 1732L7697 1683M7609 1732L7752 1900M7609 1732L7756 1697M7609 1732L7574 1626M7609 1732L7577 1736M7609 1732L7621 1790M7609 1732L7558 1521M7609 1732L7569 1503M7609 1732L7612 1587M7609 1732L7676 1647"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7702 1621h0M7697 1683h0M7752 1900h0M7756 1697h0M7574 1626h0M7577 1736h0M7621 1790h0M7558 1521h0M7569 1503h0M7612 1587h0M7676 164